'''John R. Stegner''' (born November 8, 1953) is an American attorney and a former justice of the [[Idaho Supreme Court]], having served from 2018 to 2023. He&nbsp;was previously a state district court judge for over two decades.

==Early life and education==
Born and raised in [[Grangeville, Idaho]], Stegner is the youngest of four sons of Charles and {{nowrap|June Stegner.<ref name=juneste>{{cite news |url=https://lmtribune.com/northwest/june-stegner-clarkston/article_e7419eb1-96fd-5f93-930f-caef0211fd41.html |work=Lewiston Morning Tribune |location=Idaho |agency=(obituary) |title=June Stegner, 87, Clarkston |date=July 3, 2005 |accessdate=August 29, 2018}}</ref><ref name=paobit>{{cite news |url=https://lmtribune.com/obituaries/charles-b-stegner-stegner-grain-president/article_f8ec6fce-eebc-50ee-80a4-a6751348486d.html |work=Lewiston Morning Tribune |location=Idaho |agency=(obituary) |title=Charles B. Stegner, 79, Stegner Grain president |date=March 8, 2005 |accessdate=August 29, 2018}}</ref>}} He earned his [[Bachelor of Arts]] degree in 1977 from [[Whitman College]] and his [[Juris Doctor]] from the [[University of Idaho College of Law]] in 1982.<ref name="Martindale">{{Cite web |url=https://www.martindale.com/moscow/idaho/john-r-stegner-899145-a/ |title=Hon. John R. Stegner Judge Profile on Martindale.com |website=www.martindale.com |language=en |access-date=2018-07-21}}</ref>

==Career==
Stegner was in private practice in [[Lewiston, Idaho|Lewiston]] for twelve years with Clements, Brown & McNichols. He clerked for the late [[United States District Court for the District of Idaho|U.S. District]] Judge {{nowrap|[[Harold L. Ryan]]}} for two years and worked for the family business, Stegner Grain & Seed Co.<ref name="IdahoPress">{{Cite web |url=https://www.idahopress.com/eyeonboise/judge-john-stegner-of-moscow-appointed-to-idaho-supreme-court/article_e4573f40-7b63-5df3-ab5a-945ff759dd49.html |title=Judge John Stegner of Moscow appointed to Idaho Supreme Court |last=Russell |first=Betsy |date=2018-05-22 |website=Idaho Press |language=en |access-date=2018-07-21}}</ref>

===State judicial service===
Stegner was previously a judge of the [[Courts of Idaho|state's second judicial]] district, based in [[Moscow, Idaho|Moscow]]. Appointed by [[Phil Batt]] to succeed John Judge in November 1996,<ref>{{Cite web |url=https://www.kpvi.com/news/regional_news/judge-john-stegner-appointed-to-idaho-supreme-court/article_08ce524a-5dea-11e8-9a16-aff99f4a8bbb.html |title=Judge John Stegner Appointed To Idaho Supreme Court |last=Hanian |first=Jon |date=2018-05-22 |website=KPVI |language=en |access-date=2018-07-21}}</ref> he served on the court from 1997 to 2018.<ref name="IdahoPress" /><ref>{{Cite web |url=http://sjdc.isc.idaho.gov/CourtContactInfo.aspx |title=Court Contact Info |website=sjdc.isc.idaho.gov |publisher=2nd Judicial District |language=en-US |access-date=2018-07-21}}</ref>

===Idaho Supreme Court===
In 2017, Stegner was considered for a vacancy on the state supreme court created by the retirement of [[Daniel T. Eismann|Dan Eismann]], but Governor [[Butch Otter]] appointed [[G. Richard Bevan|Richard&nbsp;Bevan]] in late August.<ref name="IdahoPress" /> Another vacancy soon arose with the sudden retirement of [[Warren Jones (Idaho judge)|Warren Jones]], and Otter appointed Stegner on May 22, 2018. He was selected from among four finalists named by the Idaho Judicial Council after public interviews with 14 applicants, including six sitting judges.<ref name="IdahoPress" /> Stegner was the only justice on the state's highest court from [[Idaho panhandle|northern]] Idaho,<ref>{{Cite web|last=Dawson|first=James|title=Moscow Judge John Stegner Appointed To Idaho Supreme Court|date=22 May 2018 |url=http://boisestatepublicradio.org/post/moscow-judge-john-stegner-appointed-idaho-supreme-court|access-date=2018-07-21|language=en}}</ref> and the first in several decades. He&nbsp;ran unopposed in the 2020 election, held during the state primary in the spring.

In May 2023, Stenger sent a letter to Governor [[Brad Little]], announcing his upcoming retirement from the court on October 31<!--, 2023--> and his return to private practice.<ref>{{Cite web |date=May 2, 2023 |title=Idaho Supreme Court Justice John Stegner to Retire |url=https://isc.idaho.gov/sites/default/files/press-release-documents/20230502-stegner-retirement.pdf |access-date=May 10, 2023 |website=State of Idaho Judicial Branch}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |date=May 1, 2023 |title=Letter to Governor |url=https://isc.idaho.gov/files/J-Stegner-letter-to-Gov.pdf |access-date=May 10, 2023}}</ref>

==Politics==
Although a [[Republican Party (United States)|Republican]] for much of his life, in early 2026 Stegner announced that he was to contest the [[2026 Idaho gubernatorial election]] as an independent.<ref>{{cite web |last1=DeGroot |first1=Gerard |title=My College Roommate is Running for Governor |url=https://gerarddegroot.substack.com/p/my-college-roommate-is-running-for |website=Past and Present |access-date=24 June 2026}}</ref> 

